WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:02.627
The Greek gods are notorious
for petty squabbling,

00:00:02.711 --> 00:00:05.088
brutal punishments, and
meddling in people's love lives.

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:07.048
But in the myth of
Prometheus, an immortal

00:00:07.132 --> 00:00:09.843
makes the ultimate sacrifice
for the sake of humankind.

00:00:09.926 --> 00:00:12.887
A wily Titan who stole
fire from the gods

00:00:12.971 --> 00:00:15.598
and gave it to humans,
Prometheus represents risk,

00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:18.351
progress, and generally
sticking it to the man, Zeus,

00:00:18.435 --> 00:00:20.854
who usually didn't take
kindly to any of those things.

00:00:20.937 --> 00:00:24.524
Across world myth,
we find fire-seizing rebels

00:00:24.607 --> 00:00:27.944
who defy the defying order
and ignite human potential.

00:00:28.027 --> 00:00:30.363
Some are punished
while some are praised.

00:00:30.447 --> 00:00:33.658
But what's so shocking about
stealing a little spark?

00:00:33.742 --> 00:00:35.869
Prometheus is often
portrayed as a trickster.

00:00:35.952 --> 00:00:38.121
But is that a fair depiction?

00:00:38.204 --> 00:00:40.081
I think, and I'll die
on this cliff,

00:00:40.165 --> 00:00:41.541
when you look closer,

00:00:41.624 --> 00:00:43.918
Prometheus is a more
complex character

00:00:44.002 --> 00:00:45.211
than you might expect.

00:00:45.295 --> 00:00:47.005
[bright music]

00:00:50.091 --> 00:00:52.343
To understand the
significance of Prometheus,

00:00:52.427 --> 00:00:56.014
we have to go way back to before
the creation of humankind.

00:00:56.097 --> 00:00:57.849
At this time, two groups of gods

00:00:57.932 --> 00:01:00.310
were battling for
control of the cosmos.

00:01:00.393 --> 00:01:02.103
The Titans, led by Cronos,

00:01:02.187 --> 00:01:04.314
were the divine children
of heaven and Earth.

00:01:04.397 --> 00:01:06.107
Shout out to the Gaia episode.

00:01:06.191 --> 00:01:07.859
The Olympians, led by Zeus,

00:01:07.942 --> 00:01:10.779
were a younger generation
of gods hungry for power.

00:01:10.862 --> 00:01:13.448
These two mighty groups
seemed equally matched.

00:01:13.531 --> 00:01:16.284
But the Titan Prometheus,
whose name means foresight,

00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:18.620
saw that the Olympians
would eventually triumph.

00:01:18.703 --> 00:01:20.622
He persuaded his
brother Epimetheus

00:01:20.705 --> 00:01:22.999
to switch sides with him
just in time.

00:01:23.082 --> 00:01:24.959
Sure enough,
Zeus defeated the Titans,

00:01:25.043 --> 00:01:27.462
casting them into eternal hell.

00:01:27.545 --> 00:01:29.547
As a token of his thanks,
Zeus gave the brothers

00:01:29.631 --> 00:01:32.509
an important task--
creating life on Earth.

00:01:32.592 --> 00:01:34.093
Epimetheus formed the animals,

00:01:34.177 --> 00:01:35.804
while Prometheus shaped
the first humans from mud.

00:01:35.887 --> 00:01:38.515
He made them
in the image of the gods,

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:40.809
but their bodies
were merely mortal.

00:01:40.892 --> 00:01:42.644
Zeus saw these new-formed
humans as puny,

00:01:42.727 --> 00:01:45.897
only good for worshiping
gods from afar.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:48.191
But Prometheus
recognizes the dignity

00:01:48.274 --> 00:01:49.275
in his fragile creations,

00:01:49.359 --> 00:01:50.777
and he wanted
to give them an advantage.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:54.322
When Zeus decreed that
humans should sacrifice

00:01:54.405 --> 00:01:57.325
half an animal to the gods,
Prometheus killed an animal

00:01:57.408 --> 00:01:59.744
and presented it in two parts.

00:01:59.828 --> 00:02:01.538
Zeus chose the meatier part,

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:03.790
leaving the skin
and bones for mortals.

00:02:03.873 --> 00:02:06.334
But when he examined the meat,
he saw that Prometheus

00:02:06.417 --> 00:02:07.919
had hidden the succulent flesh

00:02:08.002 --> 00:02:09.546
under the less attractive parts,

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:12.257
leaving only gristle
for the gods.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:14.843
Furious, Zeus withheld
fire from the Earth,

00:02:14.926 --> 00:02:17.470
leaving the first
humans hungry and cold.

00:02:17.554 --> 00:02:20.515
But this only made Prometheus
more inclined to help us.

00:02:20.598 --> 00:02:22.851
Scaling Mount Olympus,
he lit a torch from the sun god

00:02:22.934 --> 00:02:25.728
Apollo's chariot, hid it
in a hollow fennel stalk,

00:02:25.812 --> 00:02:28.106
and smuggled it
back down to Earth.

00:02:28.189 --> 00:02:31.192
With fire, Prometheus
bestowed a powerful tool.

00:02:31.276 --> 00:02:33.903
Now humans can nourish
and care for themselves.

00:02:33.987 --> 00:02:37.574
But it also meant they could
forge weapons and wage war.

00:02:37.657 --> 00:02:39.367
They learned new
skills, lived longer,

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:41.369
and traveled to faraway lands,

00:02:41.452 --> 00:02:44.372
ultimately ruling above
all other living things.

00:02:44.455 --> 00:02:47.500
Some on Olympus saw Prometheus
as a triumphant trailblazer

00:02:47.584 --> 00:02:49.919
who gave mortals
the ability to progress.

00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:51.796
Others viewed him
as a shadowy figure

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:54.841
who unlocked humans' appetite
for greed and conflict.

00:02:54.924 --> 00:02:58.011
And others denounced him
as a supreme troublemaker

00:02:58.094 --> 00:02:59.804
who needed to be taken down.

00:02:59.888 --> 00:03:02.473
You can probably guess
what camp Zeus was in.

00:03:02.557 --> 00:03:06.060
As punishment, Zeus bound
Prometheus to a remote cliff.

00:03:06.144 --> 00:03:08.187
Every day, vultures
swooped down on him

00:03:08.271 --> 00:03:09.230
and tore out his liver.

00:03:09.314 --> 00:03:11.566
And every night,
his liver grew back,

00:03:11.649 --> 00:03:13.359
ready to be devoured again.

00:03:13.443 --> 00:03:16.654
In some versions, Prometheus
is trapped for eternity.

00:03:16.738 --> 00:03:19.324
In others, Zeus eventually
allows Heracles to free him,

00:03:19.407 --> 00:03:22.201
but only because he
needs Prometheus's help.

00:03:22.285 --> 00:03:23.953
Despite his grizzly punishment,

00:03:24.037 --> 00:03:26.873
we rarely see Prometheus
expressing remorse.

00:03:26.956 --> 00:03:29.417
In Hesiod's "Theogony,"
he's a gleeful trickster

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:31.711
who happily flouts
Zeus's authority.

00:03:31.794 --> 00:03:34.130
In a series of dramas usually
ascribed to Aeschylus,

00:03:34.213 --> 00:03:37.550
he's a more cerebral figure
who waxes philosophical

00:03:37.634 --> 00:03:38.885
about the boundary
between heaven and Earth.

00:03:38.968 --> 00:03:42.472
And in the poetry of Ovid,
he's a visionary.

00:03:42.555 --> 00:03:44.641
According to Ovid,
Prometheus shaped humans

00:03:44.724 --> 00:03:47.560
with upturned faces so they
could look towards the skies,

00:03:47.644 --> 00:03:50.605
and upright,
raise their face to the stars.

00:03:50.688 --> 00:03:52.565
Here, it's Prometheus
that gives humanity

00:03:52.649 --> 00:03:53.858
our thirst for knowledge

00:03:53.942 --> 00:03:56.736
and the ability to
advance civilization.

00:03:56.819 --> 00:03:59.364
These myths imagine
the transformation of fire

00:03:59.447 --> 00:04:01.658
from a divine
or mysterious entity

00:04:01.741 --> 00:04:04.702
into something that can be
hoarded, stolen, and shared.

00:04:04.786 --> 00:04:07.622
But fire is never
an ordinary resource.

00:04:07.705 --> 00:04:11.250
Rather, it gives humans the
power to light their own way.

00:04:11.334 --> 00:04:12.961
Fire thieves are
dangerous figures

00:04:13.044 --> 00:04:16.547
because they suggest that divine
authority is not absolute.

00:04:16.631 --> 00:04:17.590
It can be tricked, twisted,

00:04:17.674 --> 00:04:20.468
and in some cases,
seized for ourselves.

00:04:20.551 --> 00:04:22.971
For transgressing the boundary
between heaven and Earth,

00:04:23.054 --> 00:04:26.140
several Promethean figures
suffered grizzly fates.

00:04:26.224 --> 00:04:27.308
Dating to the Bronze Age,

00:04:27.392 --> 00:04:29.644
a Georgian myth
tells of the hero Amirani,

00:04:29.727 --> 00:04:32.939
known for outsmarting
dragons and battling giants.

00:04:33.022 --> 00:04:35.024
His downfall comes
when he tells humans

00:04:35.108 --> 00:04:36.776
the secrets of metalwork,

00:04:36.859 --> 00:04:39.362
which had previously been
reserved for the gods.

00:04:39.445 --> 00:04:41.948
As punishment, Amirani
is chained to a cliff

00:04:42.031 --> 00:04:43.574
in the Caucasus Mountains

00:04:43.658 --> 00:04:46.327
where an eagle devours
his liver every day.

00:04:46.411 --> 00:04:49.205
Amirani does have a trusty
dog who gnaws at his chains,

00:04:49.288 --> 00:04:52.041
but every year, the gods
come back to repair it.

00:04:52.125 --> 00:04:54.168
The Vainakh people
of the North Caucasus

00:04:54.252 --> 00:04:56.462
also told of a demigod Pkharmat,

00:04:56.546 --> 00:04:58.131
who stole fire from the heavens.

00:04:58.214 --> 00:05:00.466
He pitied the humans,
known as Narts,

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:03.344
who had nothing but
cold milk and raw food.

00:05:03.428 --> 00:05:05.471
The only source of fire
belonged to the god

00:05:05.555 --> 00:05:09.559
of skies and storms, Sela,
who hoarded it in his chariot.

00:05:09.642 --> 00:05:11.310
With the help of
Sela's wife Sata,

00:05:11.394 --> 00:05:12.812
Pkharmat stole a blazing flame.

00:05:12.895 --> 00:05:15.648
For all this, he was--
you guessed it--

00:05:15.732 --> 00:05:19.110
chained to a cliff and
eaten by scavenger birds.

00:05:19.193 --> 00:05:21.529
Crucially, Pkharmat
carried fire down to Earth

00:05:21.612 --> 00:05:22.822
in a hollow reed.

00:05:22.905 --> 00:05:25.241
Eventually, the fire burned
through its container

00:05:25.324 --> 00:05:28.369
and made tiny holes,
creating the first reed pipe.

00:05:28.453 --> 00:05:32.331
Thus, he brings not only fire
but music to humanity.

00:05:32.415 --> 00:05:35.209
In this example, a Promethean
figure is associated

00:05:35.293 --> 00:05:37.795
with creativity
as well as progress.

00:05:37.879 --> 00:05:40.548
Although, while some people
refuse to recognize it,

00:05:40.631 --> 00:05:43.051
Prometheus was
a creative himself.

00:05:43.134 --> 00:05:45.428
Potter, activist,
artist, prankster,

00:05:45.511 --> 00:05:46.763
whatever you want to call him,

00:05:46.846 --> 00:05:48.681
it's hard to ignore his agency,

00:05:48.765 --> 00:05:51.726
and that agency
inspires humanity.

00:05:51.809 --> 00:05:54.771
The interpretation of Prometheus
as an imaginative spirit

00:05:54.854 --> 00:05:57.523
was particularly popular
with the Romantics,

00:05:57.607 --> 00:05:58.858
a group of writers,
artists, and intellectuals

00:05:58.941 --> 00:06:01.819
who saw subjectivity,
individualism,

00:06:01.903 --> 00:06:03.738
and emotional freedom
as paramount

00:06:03.821 --> 00:06:05.615
during the 19th century.

00:06:05.698 --> 00:06:08.659
Mary Shelley's husband Percy
wrote the romantic drama

00:06:08.743 --> 00:06:11.871
"Prometheus Unbound," in
which the Titan is a hero

00:06:11.954 --> 00:06:14.582
who spreads a message
of love and empowerment.

00:06:14.665 --> 00:06:18.044
His friend, the poet Lord Byron,
also "stanned" Prometheus.

00:06:18.127 --> 00:06:20.880
He wrote, "The godlike
crime was to be kind

00:06:20.963 --> 00:06:23.341
and strengthen man
with his own mind."

00:06:23.424 --> 00:06:26.552
Mary Shelley imagined Prometheus
as a more cautionary figure

00:06:26.636 --> 00:06:29.305
in "Frankenstein,"
in which the titular scientist

00:06:29.388 --> 00:06:30.598
makes a humanoid figure

00:06:30.681 --> 00:06:31.891
and just can't deal
with his creation.

00:06:31.974 --> 00:06:35.186
The novel is subtitled
"The Modern Prometheus,"

00:06:35.269 --> 00:06:38.523
suggesting the danger of
tampering with the natural order

00:06:38.606 --> 00:06:41.943
and the disastrous results of
misdirected human ambition.

00:06:42.026 --> 00:06:45.363
To this day, Prometheus is
a shorthand for progress.

00:06:45.446 --> 00:06:47.615
A quick Google will tell
you that a lot of companies

00:06:47.698 --> 00:06:50.034
like to reference
the original disruptor.

00:06:50.118 --> 00:06:52.370
But while billionaires who
want to fly to the moon

00:06:52.453 --> 00:06:55.373
might market themselves as
pathbreaking Prometheans,

00:06:55.456 --> 00:06:57.583
what sets them apart
from the real deal

00:06:57.667 --> 00:06:58.793
is that Prometheus
was always fighting

00:06:58.876 --> 00:07:02.213
for those less powerful
than himself.

00:07:02.296 --> 00:07:04.757
In their ancient mythical forms,
Promethean figures

00:07:04.841 --> 00:07:07.969
imbue humans with the
potential to reach new heights.

00:07:08.052 --> 00:07:10.596
But they also stand in for
our hopes and anxieties

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:13.141
about what humans
do with their power.

00:07:13.224 --> 00:07:15.560
Many also remind us
that with great power

00:07:15.643 --> 00:07:17.311
comes great responsibility.

00:07:18.437 --> 00:07:21.482
Accessibility provided by the
U.S. Department of Education.

